From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] SystemUI: a notification is dismissed by a button, not by a swipe

Measured on V86 (2026-10-27), with the row's real bounds read out of
`uiautomator dump` before each try and the swipe aimed at its middle:

  | gesture            | 400 ms | 600 ms | 900 ms |
  |--------------------|--------|--------|--------|
  | 700 px, either way | gone   |        | stays  |
  | 500 px, either way |        | stays  |        |

Direction makes no difference; the clock does. 700 px is well past the 0.4 of
the width patch 0057 lowered the threshold to, so the distance is not what
decides it. A frame on this panel takes 134-463 ms, `VelocityTracker` keeps
only the last 100 ms, and a reader on e-paper swipes slowly because they are
waiting to see the screen answer. A gesture that works at 400 ms and not at
600 ms is not a way to remove a notification; it is a coin toss, and the
losing side of it is a notification that vanishes on a scroll the reader did
not mean as a swipe.

The menu the gesture shares the row with cannot be reached either. It is
revealed by the row sliding out from under it, and since 0057 the row does not
follow the finger at all (rule 6 of docs/design/eink-ui.md), so
`isMenuVisible()` is never true and every predicate that asks for it is dead.
What is left of the long press still opens the notification's settings, and a
long press is a timer rather than a gesture -- the one thing on this panel
that cannot be mistimed.

So the gesture goes and a button takes its place. AOSP already inflates one
into every row -- `@id/veto`, 48dp wide and 0dp high, pushed off the end by a
margin of -80dp, referred to from nowhere in SystemUI 8.1. It is given the
cross SystemUI already draws (`ic_close`), the 56dp rule 7 asks for at the
edge of the screen, and the click `StatusBar.bindRow()` has always put on
every row: `performDismiss()` -> `performRemoveNotification()`, which is where
the shade's own "clear all" ends too. Nothing new reaches the notification
manager. It goes at the top right because that half of the header is empty:
the expand chevron is laid out inline after the text, measured at x 270-360 of
1072.

What the cross is drawn from is `canViewBeDismissed()`, so the ongoing USB and
adb notices do not carry one that would do nothing. The answer is asked in
three places. One is in `setHideSensitive()`, right after `mShowingPublic` is
assigned and above that method's early return, and that one call covers every
view state the stack applies: `ExpandableViewState.applyToView()` and
`animateTo()` both call `setHideSensitive()` on every row unconditionally,
whether or not the row's sensitivity changed. The other two cover moments a
row's answer changes with no state pass behind them -- `onNotificationUpdated()`
and the end of `onChildrenCountChanged()`. That last one is at the end on
purpose:
`isClearable()` reads `mIsSummaryWithChildren`, which that method reassigns on
its first line, so updating at the top would draw the cross from the count the
row came in with, and a group that has just gained an ongoing child would
carry a cross that does nothing -- on this panel, a tap that costs a refresh
and changes nothing. The click asks `canViewBeDismissed()` again rather than
trusting what was drawn.

The action list's background changes with it. `#EEEEEE` is one of this panel's
sixteen levels away from white, which rule 1 names as unusable for marking a
region; and as an opaque band the full width of the row it was painted over
the outline that 0059 draws, leaving the card's edge broken for the height of
the actions -- measured on V86, not one black pixel at the row's left edge
there. White is the colour the row already is, so the band stops being a
region of its own.

White and not transparent, which is what a first version of this patch wrote
and what looks exactly the same on a white row. `notification_action_list` is
not only a background. `NotificationColorUtil.resolveContrastColor()` reads it
as the background an application's accent colour is measured against, and
`resolveActionBarColor()` returns it as a colour in its own right:

    final int actionBg = context.getColor(
            com.android.internal.R.color.notification_action_list);
    color = NotificationColorUtil.ensureLargeTextContrast(color, actionBg, isDark);

`ColorUtilsFromCompat.calculateContrast()` documents that background as opaque,
answers a translucent one with `Log.wtf`, and then measures against the
luminance of its RGB with the alpha dropped -- for `#00000000`, the luminance
of black. The colour that comes out is not what suffers: the next line measures
the same accent against the row's real background at 4.5:1, which is stricter
than this call's 3:1, so a wrong reference here is overruled there. What it
costs is the log. Measured on V90: twenty-odd entries of 2.3 to 2.5 KB in a
single boot (`dumpsys dropbox --print`), entered from
`Notification$Builder.resolveContrastColor` and from
`ExpandableNotificationRow.updateNotificationColor` -- a stack written to
storage every time a notification is built or expanded, for a value that was
only ever meant to be the colour of a band. White is that colour, and it is
opaque.

Only that one value changes in `colors.xml`, and nothing is written above the
entry. A first build of this patch carried seven lines of explanation there,
and the export's `boot_image.sha256` over the 45 ART boot files came out
`ba857c7b91108dd45674ae86ca31eac65f6127a56a3abff4bb5ec1672086ccfc` against the
`73db7fc9d00b36b16b0295034c2c0f49eccf92683a33ee4058c0c6d92123c3d4` that V80
through V86 all share; with the comment moved here it is `73db7fc9...` again.
That is the field the payload manifest carries and the installer reads to
decide whether Android compiles the applications in /data again.
`aapt2` copies a comment that sits directly above an
entry into `com.android.internal.R` as Javadoc; every class declared after it
in the generated `R.java` moves down by those lines; and the line numbers
their constructors carry are in `framework.jar`, which is in the boot image. A
boot image that moves costs every app in `/data` its machine code. So the
explanation lives here, in the patch, and the resource file keeps the shape
AOSP gave it.

--- a/core/res/res/values/colors.xml
+++ b/core/res/res/values/colors.xml
